1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"blind_id\":\"C034\",\"coverage\":[{\"point\":1,\"credit\":1,\"reason\":\"States that costly or illiquid holdings raise hedging costs and uncertainty, so market makers 'may quote wider'. It also says basket trading, financing, fees, operational constraints and inventory risk in creation/redemption 'can affect quotes'.\"},{\"point\":2,\"credit\":1,\"reason\":\"'quoted spread reflects the cost and risk of providing immediacy': the spread compensates market-maker hedging, inventory and basket costs. It gives no numerical ranking or universal levels.\"},{\"point\":3,\"credit\":1,\"reason\":\"Says volume does not capture 'underlying liquidity, or dealers' ability to hedge and process baskets', which links to the underlying market and creation/redemption. Gives the example that a lightly traded ETF can have competitive quotes if its holdings and hedges are liquid. Also mentions current order-book depth (optional).\"}],\"central_error\":null,\"local_error\":null,\"grade\":\"correct\",\"rationale\":\"All three required minimums are covered. The answer ties spreads to underlying liquidity, hedging and creation/redemption costs, and treats the spread as compensation for supplying immediacy. It explains that volume is incomplete because liquidity also comes from the underlying basket and the hedging/basket process. It contains no central error, no material falsehood, and no ranking or universal spread levels.\"}","ttft_ms":4144,"type":"result","duration_ms":8053,"uuid":"637fe4d0-fc1e-42c2-a233-f6aff52aeb74","ttft_stream_ms":1114,"time_to_request_ms":57,"first_content_frame_ms":1114,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
